L["baptist"] = """About four hundred years had passed since the last prophet spoke in Israel. Then, in the fifteenth year of the emperor Tiberius, the word of God came to a man named John in the wilderness. John was the son of Zechariah, a priest, and Elizabeth. His birth had been a miracle, since his parents were old and had no children. An angel had told Zechariah that his son would prepare the way for the Lord.

John lived out in the desert. He wore clothes made of camel's hair with a leather belt around his waist, and he ate locusts and wild honey. He looked like the prophet Elijah, and that was no accident. He went into the area around the Jordan River, preaching a baptism of repentance for the forgiveness of sins.

His message was short. Repent, for the kingdom of heaven has come near. Matthew says John was the one Isaiah spoke of when he wrote, a voice of one calling in the wilderness, prepare the way for the Lord, make straight paths for him. In the ancient world, a road was cleared before a king arrived. John's job was to get people ready for the King.

People came from Jerusalem, from all of Judea and the whole region of the Jordan. Confessing their sins, they were baptized by him in the river. John didn't tell them what they wanted to hear. When he saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to be baptized, he said, you brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath? Produce fruit in keeping with repentance. Don't think you can say to yourselves, we have Abraham as our father. Being born into the right family was not enough.

The crowds asked him, what should we do then? John said, anyone who has two shirts should share with the one who has none, and anyone who has food should do the same. Tax collectors came and asked what to do. Don't collect any more than you are required to. Soldiers asked, and he said, don't extort money and don't accuse people falsely. Be content with your pay. His advice was practical. Repentance meant changing how you lived on Monday morning.

John was also humble. People began to wonder if he might be the Messiah. He said, I baptize you with water, but one who is more powerful than I will come, the straps of whose sandals I am not worthy to untie. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire. He must become greater, and I must become less.

Why does this matter? John was the last of the Old Testament prophets and the first voice of the New. He didn't try to attract attention to himself. He pointed beyond himself. Jesus later said that among those born of women, there has not risen anyone greater than John the Baptist.

What happened after? John baptized Jesus, and then he saw the Spirit come down on him. The next day he said, look, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world. Some of John's followers left to follow Jesus, and John was glad. Later, he told King Herod that it was wrong for him to marry his brother's wife, and Herod put him in prison. John was killed there. Jesus grieved for him.

What does this mean for us today? John's message is still the first step. We make room for God by being honest about what is wrong and turning toward him. It also looks like fairness and generosity in everyday life, not just religious talk. And John shows what a good messenger is like. He didn't say, look at me. He said, look at him.

That is why this story is so important. It says that God sent someone ahead to get his people ready, and that the way to meet the King is through repentance. And it points straight to Jesus, the Lamb of God."""
